// batch z: chapultepec, solferino, puebla, antietam, gettysburg, nanquim1864
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "voltigeurs": "A light infantry regiment of the American army, created in 1847 for the war against Mexico.",
  "escada de assalto": "A wooden ladder that attackers carry to climb walls and parapets. The attack stalls if it is delayed, and whoever carries it is exposed to fire.",
  "cadete": "A student at a military school, usually a teenager or young adult studying to become an officer.",
  "contra-ataque": "An attack by the defender against an attacker who has just advanced and is tired and disorganized.",
  "estrada funda": "A dirt road worn down by use and rain, which works as a natural trench.",
  "união": "The Northern states, which stayed loyal to the government of the United States in the Civil War. Also called 'the North'.",
  "confederação": "The eleven Southern states that seceded from the United States in 1861 and formed a country of their own, in defense of slavery. Also called 'the South'.",
  "proclamação de emancipação": "Lincoln's decree declaring the enslaved people of the rebel states free. The preliminary version came out on 22 September 1862 and took effect on 1 January 1863.",
  "saliente": "A stretch of the line that juts out ahead of the rest and can be attacked from more than one side. It looks strong, but is hard to defend.",
  "cavalaria desmontada": "Cavalry that fights on foot: the soldiers get off their horses and fire carbines, while the animals stay behind.",
  "risorgimento": "'Resurgence', in Italian: the movement that unified Italy in the 19th century. The Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861.",
  "quadrilátero": "The four Austrian fortresses of northern Italy (Mantua, Peschiera, Verona and Legnago). Whoever shut himself in there could hold out a long time, and attacking them was extremely costly.",
  "cruz vermelha": "An organization founded in Geneva, in 1863, to aid the war wounded of any side. Its symbol is a red cross on a white background.",
  "taiping": "A religious and political movement created by Hong Xiuquan, which mixed Christianity with ideas of its own. 'Taiping' means 'great peace'. It set up a state that rivaled the Qing Empire.",
  "exército de hunan": "A regional army formed by Zeng Guofan with volunteers from Hunan province, because the empire's regular troops had failed against the Taiping. Also called the Xiang Army."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  chapultepec: {
    war: "Mexican-American War (1846–1848)",
    hook: "A castle on top of a hill, a military school inside it and a legend that Mexico repeats to this day.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1845, the United States annexed Texas, which had split from Mexico in 1836. The border was disputed, and in April 1846 the war began. The American president also wanted California and the territory between it and Texas, which were Mexican.",
        "In 1847, the American general Winfield Scott landed a small army on the coast and marched inland, toward the capital. He won one battle after another, but each one cost men he could not replace. By the time he got near Mexico City, he had about 7,200 soldiers.",
        { h: "The road to Chapultepec" },
        { timeline: [
          ["March 1847", "Scott takes the port of Veracruz and begins the march to the capital."],
          ["April to August 1847", "Victories at Cerro Gordo, Contreras and Churubusco. The capital is a few kilometers away."],
          ["8 September", "Molino del Rey, a costly battle for the Americans, one step from Chapultepec Hill."],
          ["11 September", "Council of war: Scott decides to attack Chapultepec Castle."],
          ["12 and 13 September", "Bombardment and assault on the hill."],
          ["14 September", "The Americans enter Mexico City."]
        ]},
        "The castle stood on a hill about 60 meters high, at the edge of the city. It had been built in the 1780s as a luxury residence and, since 1841, housed Mexico's Military College. A wall about 4.5 meters high and 1.5 kilometers long surrounded the base. Whoever took it could reach the gates of the city."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Winfield Scott" },
        "A general in the American army, a veteran of the War of 1812. On 11 September, he gathered his officers to decide where to attack. Almost all preferred the southern gates. Scott wanted the castle, and only General Twiggs agreed at first. General Franklin Pierce, who also preferred the south, changed his vote after a speech by P. G. T. Beauregard, and Scott decided to attack the castle.",
        { h: "Antonio López de Santa Anna" },
        "President and commander of the Mexican army, he claimed to have some 25,000 men in the region. He expected the main attack from the south, and only realized his mistake when the Americans were already climbing the hill. After the defeat, he was blamed for the loss of the capital and left the presidency on 26 September.",
        { h: "Nicolás Bravo and the defenders" },
        "General Bravo commanded the castle garrison: about 830 men and seven cannons. The San Blas Battalion, commanded by Felipe Santiago Xicoténcatl, helped hold the hill, and Xicoténcatl died there. On the 11th, Bravo sent most of the roughly 200 [[cadets|cadete]] of the college out of the castle, but about 50 stayed, with recently graduated former students and staff. The numbers vary widely by author: from 47 to a few hundred."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers are uncertain. Scott had about 7,200 men in the whole army, and only part of them attacked the hill. The castle's defenders were some 830, against a larger Mexican army, but scattered around the city. The Americans had better artillery and better-trained soldiers. The Mexicans had the hill, the wall and the advantage of being at home. In 1847, the bells of the city's churches were melted down to make cannonballs, and the defenders reinforced the walls with sandbags, beams and shallow ditches.",
        { h: "The bombardment" },
        { formation: true },
        "At 7 a.m. on 12 September, the American artillery opened fire on the castle and kept going all day, stopping only at dusk. The next morning, it resumed at dawn.",
        { h: "The assault of 13 September" },
        "Around 8 a.m., the bombardment stopped and Scott ordered the infantry to advance in three columns. The attackers reached the foot of the wall and stalled: the [[ladders|escada de assalto]] had not yet arrived. There Colonel Truman Ransom, of the 9th Regiment, died, and General Gideon Pillow was wounded in the foot and handed over command.",
        "Quitman sent reinforcements, and the brigade carrying the ladders arrived by the west slope. The attackers climbed the wall, and the [[voltigeurs]] were the first to plant a flag on the parapet. Near 9 a.m., Bravo surrendered and the American flag went up over the castle.",
        { h: "The Boy Heroes" },
        "Six defenders are remembered by Mexico as the Niños Héroes: Lieutenant Juan de la Barrera and the cadets Agustín Melgar, Juan Escutia, Vicente Suárez, Francisco Márquez and Fernando Montes de Oca, aged about 13 to 20. According to tradition, they refused to leave the castle and died fighting. In the best-known version, Escutia wrapped himself in the Mexican flag and threw himself from the heights so it would not be captured.",
        { note: "The story of the cadets is one of Mexico's strongest national myths. Recent historians say there is no proof of Escutia's leap, and that he may have been a 20-year-old soldier of the San Blas Battalion, not a cadet. The official version gained strength from 1852. The number of cadets present and the identification of the remains found in 1947 are also disputed. About 40 surviving cadets were taken prisoner." },
        { h: "After the hill" },
        "With the castle taken, Scott sent two columns along the causeways that lead to the city. At 1:20 p.m., Quitman opened the Belén gate, which was a feint. Around 4 p.m., General Worth began advancing along the San Cosme causeway and, at 6 p.m., broke through the gate, and the defenders scattered, many to the Ciudadela, the capital's main fort. At San Cosme, Lieutenant Ulysses Grant took a howitzer up into a church tower.",
        "In the early hours of 14 September, Santa Anna pulled the army out of the city. By 7 a.m., the American flag was already over the Ciudadela."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to English Wikipedia, the Americans had 138 dead, 673 wounded and 29 missing in the fighting of those days. On the Mexican side, some 200 dead and 632 wounded or captured, plus about 3,700 prisoners in all. Scott wrote that some 20,000 Mexican soldiers deserted.",
        "Mexico City fell on 14 September. The war ended with the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo, signed on 2 February 1848: Mexico recognized the loss of Texas and ceded California and much of what is now the American West, nearly half its territory, for 15 million dollars.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "It was the battle that decided the war, and Mexico never forgot the price. On 13 September, the country remembers the Niños Héroes. On the American side, the Marines' hymn ('From the Halls of Montezuma') refers to the campaign.",
        "Many young officers who fought there became generals in the American Civil War, on both sides: Ulysses Grant, Robert E. Lee, Thomas Jackson, James Longstreet and George Pickett. Almost all of them appear in the other battles of this collection.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The San Patricios: a group of Americans who deserted the army during Taylor's campaign formed the Saint Patrick's Battalion, which fought for Mexico. Those captured at Churubusco were hanged, about 30 of them, at the moment the flag of the United States went up over the castle, according to English Wikipedia." },
        { note: "In 1947, the American president Harry Truman laid a wreath at the monument to the Niños Héroes, a hundred years after the battle." },
        { note: "The tradition that the red stripe on the trousers of the American Marines recalls the blood shed at Chapultepec is false: only 7 of the roughly 400 to 450 Marines died there." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  solferino: {
    war: "Second Italian War of Independence (1859)",
    hook: "The battle nobody planned, and whose horror gave birth to the Red Cross.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1859, Italy did not yet exist as a country. The region was a mosaic of kingdoms, duchies and Papal States, and much of the north, Lombardy and Venetia, belonged to the Austrian Empire. The Kingdom of Piedmont-Sardinia, ruled by Victor Emmanuel II, was the only strong and independent Italian state, and wanted to expel the Austrians. Its prime minister, Count Cavour, knew it could not do so alone.",
        "On 21 July 1858, Cavour met in secret with Napoleon III, emperor of France, at Plombières. They agreed that France would help Piedmont against Austria, in exchange for Nice and Savoy. The treaty of alliance was signed in January 1859. Cavour provoked Vienna with maneuvers on the border, and Austria sent an ultimatum on 23 April. The war began three days later.",
        { h: "The road to Solferino" },
        { timeline: [
          ["20 May", "First major clash, at Montebello."],
          ["4 June", "The Austrians lose at Magenta, and Emperor Franz Joseph personally takes command in place of Gyulai."],
          ["June", "The Franco-Piedmontese enter Milan and advance east."],
          ["22 and 23 June", "The two armies cross rivers, toward each other."],
          ["24 June", "They meet and fight: it is the Battle of Solferino."]
        ]},
        "After Magenta, the Austrian general Gyulai fell back east of the Mincio River and was replaced by the emperor himself. Franz Joseph, 28 years old, decided to cross the river again and attack. Napoleon III was also advancing, and the two armies bumped into each other, in a place that nobody had chosen."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Napoleon III, 50 years old" },
        "Nephew of Napoleon Bonaparte and emperor of the French, he had little battle experience and relied on veteran commanders from Algeria and the Crimea, such as MacMahon, Canrobert, Niel and Baraguey d'Hilliers. He had an old connection to the Italian cause: in his youth, he had taken part in revolts in Italy.",
        { h: "Victor Emmanuel II, 39 years old" },
        "King of Piedmont-Sardinia, he commanded the Sardinian army alongside the minister of war, La Marmora. His kingdom had been defeated by Austria in 1849, and the war of 1859 was the chance to change that result.",
        { h: "Franz Joseph, 28 years old" },
        "Emperor of Austria since the age of 18, he had taken command after Magenta. His chief of staff, Heinrich von Hess, and his adjutant Karl von Grünne, according to Wikipedia, were constantly quarreling.",
        { h: "Ludwig von Benedek" },
        "Commander of the Austrian VIII Corps, of some 39,000 men, in the north of the battlefield. At San Martino, he kept fighting even after receiving an order to fall back."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Numbers vary by source. The Austrians were about 129,000 men with 429 cannons, divided into two armies. The French had about 92,000 men, with 240 cannons, in four [[army corps|corpo de exército]] plus the [[Imperial Guard|guarda imperial]]. The Sardinians totaled some 38,500, with 80 cannons. In all, the allies had from 130,000 to 133,000 men. The two sides were almost the same size.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Low hills south of Lake Garda, between Milan and Verona. The Austrians occupied the villages of Solferino, Cavriana and Volta Mantovana, in the center, and Medole and Guidizzolo, in the south. In the north, at San Martino and Pozzolengo, the terrain was more rugged. The battle front stretched more than 15 kilometers.",
        { h: "A battle in three fights" },
        { formation: true },
        "Since neither side had a fixed plan, the battle split into three almost separate combats.",
        "In the south, at Medole, around 4 a.m., General Niel, of the French 4th Corps, met an Austrian force and attacked at once. He held a thin line, about 5 kilometers long, and with that the Austrian III, IX and XI Corps could not reinforce Solferino. Only in the afternoon, when those corps arrived in force, did he fall back from Guidizzolo, after two hours of fighting.",
        "In the center, at Solferino, the vanguard of the French 1st Corps made contact with the enemy around 4:30, and MacMahon, at 5, ran into Hungarian units. The Austrians held on all day against repeated attacks and received reinforcements from the I Corps. Napoleon III threw the divisions of the Imperial Guard into the fight. Around 2 p.m., the cemetery and the village were surrounded.",
        "In the north, at San Martino, the Sardinians faced Benedek's corps from 7 a.m. The village changed hands several times: from 7 a.m. to 5 p.m., the Piedmontese took it three times and the Austrians retook it. Benedek ignored an order to fall back and kept fighting. Only on the fourth attack, around 8 p.m., did the Sardinians keep it, and the Austrians fell back after a failed [[counterattack|contra-ataque]]. This fight tied down Benedek's corps all day and kept it from reinforcing the center.",
        "Around 3 p.m., Canrobert's 3rd Corps and the Guard attacked Cavriana, the village that anchored the Austrian center. It fell at 6 p.m. The broken center forced the entire Austrian army to fall back.",
        { h: "After it all" },
        "The Austrians withdrew to the [[Quadrilateral|quadrilátero]], the four fortresses that defended Venetia, and the campaign ended there."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Austrians had 22,310 casualties: 2,386 dead, 10,634 wounded and 9,290 missing. The allies had between 16,700 and 17,200, according to the English Wikipedia table, which gives two sets of numbers that do not match. Between the two sides, that is about 40,000 dead, wounded and missing in a single day.",
        "Napoleon III was shocked by the carnage. In addition, there was the threat of Prussian intervention and opposition from French Catholics at home. On 11 July, he signed the Armistice of Villafranca with Franz Joseph. Piedmont got Lombardy, but Venetia remained Austrian. Cavour, furious, resigned.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "In 1860, the states of central Italy joined Piedmont and France received Nice and Savoy. The Kingdom of Italy was proclaimed in 1861, and the [[Risorgimento|risorgimento]] continued on to Rome and Venice.",
        "But the most famous legacy was born of the horror. The Swiss businessman Henry Dunant was in the area and, after the battle, walked through the field, where thousands of wounded from all sides were dying without help. At Castiglione delle Stiviere, he organized care in the churches with the help of local women, who kept repeating that all men are brothers. In 1862, he published 'A Memory of Solferino', and in 1863 the [[Red Cross|cruz vermelha]] was created. In 1864 came the first Geneva Convention, which protects the wounded and those who care for them.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "It is said to have been the last great battle in history in which all the armies were under the personal command of their sovereigns: Napoleon III, Victor Emmanuel II and Franz Joseph." },
        { note: "Venetia only went to Italy in 1866, and Rome in 1870." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  puebla: {
    war: "Second French Intervention in Mexico (1861–1867)",
    hook: "The day a poorly equipped Mexican army stopped, for a year, what was said to be the best army in the world.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Mexico in 1861 was broke. After years of wars, the latest conflict, the Reform War, between liberals and conservatives, had just ended. The liberal president Benito Juárez suspended payment of the foreign debt for two years. It was the excuse for France, Spain and the United Kingdom to sign the Convention of London and send troops to collect.",
        "The three armies landed at Veracruz between the end of 1861 and January 1862. But the French had other plans. Napoleon III wanted access to Mexico's resources, mainly silver, and an allied Mexico to balance the influence of the United States, then busy with the Civil War. The Mexican conservatives, defeated in the internal war, wanted a foreign king. When it became clear that France intended to invade, Spain and the United Kingdom withdrew, in April 1862.",
        { h: "The road to Puebla" },
        { timeline: [
          ["July 1861", "Juárez suspends payment of the foreign debt."],
          ["December 1861", "Spain takes Veracruz. The French and British arrive in January."],
          ["April 1862", "The alliance breaks down. Spain and the United Kingdom withdraw, and the French go on alone."],
          ["17 April", "The conservative general Juan Almonte publishes a manifesto in favor of the French."],
          ["2 to 4 May", "The French reach Amozoc and camp within sight of Puebla."],
          ["5 May", "Battle of Puebla."]
        ]},
        "The French took Orizaba after defeating a small Mexican force, and Porfirio Díaz and Ignacio Zaragoza fell back to Puebla, a city halfway to the capital."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Ignacio Zaragoza, 33 years old" },
        "A liberal general and commander of the Mexican Army of the East. He had a little over 3,500 men and few resources. He died of typhus about four months after the battle.",
        { h: "Charles de Lorencez" },
        "A French count and general, he commanded the expeditionary corps. According to the traditional account, he underestimated the enemy. His plan was to take the forts of Loreto and Guadalupe, on the hills north of the city. Two advisers, Almonte and Antonio de Haro y Tamariz, advised other plans, which were ignored.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Miguel Negrete commanded the division that occupied the two forts on 4 May. Felipe Berriozábal was also among the Mexican commanders. The troops of Porfirio Díaz, who years later would be president, counterattacked."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Numbers vary: according to English Wikipedia, about 5,730 French against some 3,800 Mexicans, but other sources speak of up to 4,500 on the Mexican side. The French had more training, better weapons and heavier artillery. The Mexicans had the forts, the terrain and the motivation of defending their own country.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Puebla lay in a valley, and north of it there were two hills with forts: Loreto and Guadalupe, the higher one. Whoever took them dominated the city. The Mexicans occupied both forts and the ground around them.",
        { h: "The battle" },
        { formation: true },
        "The attack began around noon on 5 May. First, the French artillery bombarded the forts for about two hours. Then an infantry column climbed from the north side toward Fort Guadalupe.",
        "The Mexicans received the column with rifle and cannon fire, and Díaz's troops made a [[counterattack|contra-ataque]]. The French repeated the assault, and all were repelled. The most repeated count is three attacks. Many accounts speak of a heavy rain that turned the slope to mud, which made the climb harder. A charge by the Mexican cavalry kept the French infantry from regrouping.",
        "The fight lasted until about 7 p.m. The French fell back to Los Alamos and, on 8 May, to Orizaba, where they awaited reinforcements."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to English Wikipedia, the Mexicans lost 87 dead, 252 wounded and 12 missing. The French, 131 dead and 345 wounded. It is a victory with small casualties, but with great moral effect.",
        "Congress thanked Zaragoza, and the city came to be called Puebla de Zaragoza. Napoleon III sent more troops and dismissed Lorencez. General Forey returned on 16 March 1863 and besieged Puebla. The city surrendered on 17 May 1863, without ammunition or food. In June 1863, the French entered Mexico City, and in 1864 Maximilian of Austria arrived to be emperor.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The victory at Puebla delayed the French by almost a year and helped the Mexicans keep up the fight. In 1866, France began withdrawing troops, pressured by the United States and by the Prussian threat in Europe. Without them, the Empire fell. Maximilian was shot on 19 June 1867.",
        "Díaz, who escaped being captured by the French, became president in 1876.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Cinco de Mayo is not Mexico's independence day, which is 16 September. The date is celebrated mostly in the state of Puebla. In the United States, the festival grew from the 1950s and 1960s and became a celebration of Mexican culture, more popular than in Mexico itself." },
        { note: "The sources disagree on the numbers of troops and casualties: English Wikipedia gives different numbers on two different pages. Here we used those of the battle's own page." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  antietam: {
    war: "American Civil War (1861–1865), Maryland Campaign",
    hook: "The bloodiest day in United States history began with three cigars and a lost piece of paper.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1862, the United States was at war with itself. The South had formed the [[Confederacy|confederação]] to defend slavery, and the North, the [[Union|união]], was trying to bring it back. At the end of August, the Confederate general Robert E. Lee had won the Second Battle of Bull Run, near Washington. He decided to take advantage of the momentum and invade the North for the first time.",
        "On 3 September, the Army of Northern Virginia, of some 55,000 men, entered Maryland. Lee expected to be welcomed, but found many people loyal to the Union, especially in the west of the state.",
        { h: "The road to Antietam" },
        { timeline: [
          ["3 September", "Lee crosses the Potomac River and enters Maryland."],
          ["9 September", "Lee divides the army into several parts, with Special Order 191."],
          ["13 September", "Union soldiers find a copy of the order in a field, wrapped around three cigars."],
          ["14 September", "Fighting at South Mountain: the Confederates hold the enemy long enough to concentrate."],
          ["15 September", "Jackson takes the fort at Harpers Ferry with thousands of Union soldiers."],
          ["17 September", "Battle of Antietam, near Sharpsburg."]
        ]},
        "Corporal Barton Mitchell and Sergeant John Bloss, of Indiana, found the paper. It showed that Lee had divided his army. General McClellan, the Union commander, waited about 18 hours before acting. According to historians, it was a lost chance to defeat Lee for good."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "George McClellan" },
        "Commander of the Army of the Potomac, he was an excellent organizer and was adored by the soldiers. But he was cautious and always thought the enemy had more men than he did. At Antietam, he believed Lee had up to 100,000 soldiers. He sent his army corps to attack one at a time, without coordination, and left the reserves idle.",
        { h: "Robert E. Lee" },
        "The Confederate commander, he decided to stay and fight with the Potomac River at his back and a much smaller force, around one to two. He threw the whole army into the fight and held all day.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Union side, Joseph Hooker (I Corps), Joseph Mansfield (XII), Edwin Sumner (II) and Ambrose Burnside (IX). On the Confederate side, Thomas 'Stonewall' Jackson, on the left; James Longstreet, on the right; D. H. Hill, in the center; and A. P. Hill, who arrived from Harpers Ferry late in the afternoon."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Union had some 87,000 men present, but only some 72,000 available to fight, and about 53,600 entered the fight. About a quarter of the army was inexperienced. The artillery was superior, with some 275 cannons. The Confederates had from 30,600 to 37,600 men, depending on the source, with many old weapons. Illness and soldiers who fell out along the way had reduced Lee's army.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Antietam Creek was 18 to 30 meters wide, could be crossed on foot in some places, and had three stone bridges, about 1.6 kilometers from one another. The Confederate line ran along a low ridge near Sharpsburg, with stone and wooden fences and limestone outcrops. Three places became famous: Miller's cornfield, about 230 by 360 meters; the [[sunken road|estrada funda]], about 700 meters long, which worked as a natural trench; and the southern stone bridge, which would later bear Burnside's name. Behind Lee was the Potomac, and the only nearby crossing was Boteler's Ford, near Shepherdstown.",
        { h: "The battle in three time slots" },
        { formation: true },
        "The fighting was made up of three separate attacks, over the course of the day, from north to south.",
        { h: "Morning: the cornfield" },
        "Around 5:30, Hooker's I Corps attacked toward the Dunker church, which stood on a small plateau. The cornfield became a grinder: it changed hands several times, and the 12th Massachusetts Regiment lost 67% of its men. Hood's division, reinforced by brigades from Hill and Early, drove the Union back around 7, but lost about 60%. Hooker was hit in the foot by a sharpshooter and left the field.",
        "Mansfield's XII Corps attacked between 7:30 and 9. Mansfield, new to the command, was mortally wounded. Greene's division even got close to the church. Near 9, Sumner sent Sedgwick's division in close columns, and it was hit on the flank and lost more than 2,200 men. The morning ended with some 13,000 dead and wounded on both sides.",
        { h: "Midday: the sunken road" },
        "From 9:30, French's division attacked D. H. Hill's position, at the sunken road, in assaults of one brigade at a time. The first was destroyed. Then Richardson's division arrived, and the Irish Brigade attacked and lost some 540 men. Near noon, Caldwell's brigade advanced, and two New York regiments, the 61st and the 64th, took a position from which they fired along the road. The Confederate line collapsed, helped by a confusion of orders. The road cost some 5,600 dead and wounded.",
        "Franklin's VI Corps was ready to exploit the gap, but Sumner ordered a halt, and McClellan backed him. The chance passed.",
        { h: "Afternoon: the bridge" },
        "Burnside received the order to attack the bridge at 10. About 400 Georgians, under Robert Toombs, defended it from the slopes. Several regiments failed. Around 12:30, the 51st New York and the 51st Pennsylvania crossed at a run, and Toombs, fearing a flanking, fell back around 1 p.m. Burnside spent two more hours on logistics and at 3 advanced with some 8,000 men.",
        "At that moment, A. P. Hill arrived. His Light Division had marched some 27 kilometers from Harpers Ferry, crossed Boteler's Ford and entered the field at 3:30. At 3:40, the South Carolinians of Maxcy Gregg struck Burnside's flank, and the 16th Connecticut collapsed. Burnside's men fell back to the creek.",
        "McClellan still had two rested reserves, the V and VI Corps, and refused to use them. The battle ended around 5:30."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Union lost 12,410 men and the Confederates 10,316: about 22,700 dead, wounded and missing in one day, the bloodiest in American history. The Union lost about 25% of its army and the Confederacy about 31%. The generals who died or were mortally wounded were Mansfield, Richardson and Rodman, of the Union, and Branch and Starke, of the South. The nurse Clara Barton brought supplies to the field.",
        "On 18 September, the two armies stood still, Lee waiting for an attack that did not come. At night, he crossed the Potomac back. McClellan did not pursue him from 17 September to 26 October. Lincoln dismissed him on 5 November, and Burnside took over on 9 November.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "On 22 September, Lincoln issued the preliminary version of the [[Emancipation Proclamation|proclamação de emancipação]], which took effect on 1 January 1863. Secretary of State Seward had advised waiting for a real victory, and Antietam was what there was. The battle also discouraged England and France from recognizing the Confederacy, because neither wanted to look like it supported slavery.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Who won? Tactically, the battle was inconclusive, and Lee was the first to retreat, which by the technical definition makes him the loser. Strategically, it ended the invasion of the North and is seen as a turning point for the Union. That is why this battle's card does not declare a winner." },
        { note: "The famous line that General Porter refused reinforcements, saying he was saving the 'last reserve', is probably fiction, according to the historian Scott Hartwig." },
        { note: "Historians also debate Sumner's attack in the morning: most condemn it as reckless, but M. V. Armstrong argues that the reconnaissance he carried out justified it." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  gettysburg: {
    war: "American Civil War (1861–1865), Gettysburg Campaign",
    hook: "Three days in July in which the biggest battle of the American Civil War turned the game around.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In May 1863, Robert E. Lee won the Battle of Chancellorsville, in Virginia, against a much larger [[Union|união]] army. Nothing seemed able to stop the Army of Northern Virginia. But Lee had lost his best general there, Stonewall Jackson, and had to reorganize the army into three corps, under James Longstreet, Richard Ewell and A. P. Hill. The cavalry stayed under J. E. B. Stuart.",
        "Lee decided to invade the North for the second time, after Antietam. The goals were to relieve pressure on Vicksburg, on the Mississippi River, to feed the army on Northern farms, to threaten Philadelphia, Baltimore and Washington, and to strengthen the peace movement among Northerners.",
        { h: "The road to Gettysburg" },
        { timeline: [
          ["May 1863", "Lee's victory at Chancellorsville. Jackson dies later, wounded by friendly fire."],
          ["9 June", "Brandy Station, the largest cavalry battle of the war, with no clear winner."],
          ["June", "Lee marches north, into Maryland and Pennsylvania."],
          ["28 June", "Meade replaces Hooker in command of the Army of the Potomac."],
          ["1 to 3 July", "Battle of Gettysburg."]
        ]},
        "Joseph Hooker resigned in June after a quarrel over the Harpers Ferry garrison, and Lincoln chose George Meade, who took over three days before the battle. Stuart, meanwhile, left with the three best cavalry brigades on a ride around the Union army and lost contact. Lee, without the eyes of his cavalry, advanced almost blind."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Robert E. Lee, 56 years old" },
        "He came off the victory at Chancellorsville, against a Union army much larger than his own. At Gettysburg, he attacked three days in a row, and on the third insisted on the attack on the center even after Longstreet disagreed.",
        { h: "George Meade, 47 years old" },
        "A career military engineer, he had only three days in command when the battle began. He held the line and sent reinforcements to threatened points, such as Little Round Top. He was later criticized for not attacking Lee on 4 July.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Union side, John Buford, whose cavalry opened the fight; John Reynolds, of the I Corps, who died on the first day; Winfield Hancock, of the II Corps, who organized the defense; Daniel Sickles, of the III Corps, who advanced without orders; and Strong Vincent and Joshua Chamberlain, at Little Round Top. On the Confederate side, James Longstreet, who disagreed with Lee's plan, and Richard Ewell, who missed a chance on the first day."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The Union had between 93,500 and 104,000 men, with some 360 cannons. The Confederates had from 65,000 to 75,000, with some 270 cannons and 9,500 cavalrymen. Numbers vary by source.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "West of the town were three parallel ridges: Herr, McPherson and Seminary. To the south, the hills Cemetery Hill and Culp's Hill and Cemetery Ridge, a long ridge running south to the hills Little Round Top and Big Round Top. The Union line took the shape of a fishhook. Out in front of the Union line were the Devil's Den, the peach orchard, the wheatfield and the Emmitsburg road, where the fighting took place on the second day.",
        { h: "First day: 1 July" },
        { formation: true },
        "Around 7:30, two Confederate brigades of Heth ran into Buford's [[dismounted cavalry|cavalaria desmontada]], who delayed the advance with carbines. Near 10:20, Reynolds's I Corps arrived. Reynolds was killed early in the fighting. The Iron Brigade fought well and captured General Archer, but was flanked and fell back.",
        "In the afternoon, Ewell's corps arrived from the north. Around 2, the divisions of Rodes and Early flanked the I and XI Corps, and Barlow's division was crushed. Howard fell back to Cemetery Hill, and Hancock, sent by Meade, found the place strong enough to fight.",
        "Lee told Ewell to take Cemetery Hill 'if practicable'. Ewell thought it was not, and historians consider this a major missed chance.",
        { h: "Second day: 2 July" },
        "Lee's plan was to attack the Union left with Longstreet, while Ewell attacked the right. The orders only went out around 11, and Longstreet's divisions took hours to reach their positions.",
        "Meanwhile, Sickles, without orders, advanced the III Corps to the peach orchard, creating an exposed [[salient|saliente]]. The divisions of Hood and McLaws attacked. Meade sent some 20,000 men as reinforcements, including the V Corps.",
        "At Little Round Top, Strong Vincent's brigade, sent minutes earlier by Warren, held the hill against Law's men. Chamberlain's 20th Maine made the famous bayonet charge. Vincent was mortally wounded and promoted to general on his deathbed. In the wheatfield and the orchard, the III Corps was nearly destroyed, and Sickles lost a leg. The 1st Minnesota made a costly counterattack that bought time for the reinforcements to arrive.",
        "At 6, Ewell attacked Culp's Hill and East Cemetery Hill. Greene's brigade held Culp's Hill, although it lost some trenches. Hays and Avery reached the defenses of East Cemetery Hill, but fell back when Rodes did not support them.",
        { h: "Third day: 3 July" },
        "In the morning, both sides fought again for Culp's Hill, and around 11 the Union line was still intact. Lee then decided to attack the center, at Cemetery Ridge, defended by the II Corps, with Pickett's division and six brigades from Hill. Longstreet said there were too few men, but Lee insisted.",
        "Around 1 p.m., 150 to 170 Confederate cannons opened fire. The Union artillery waited, then answered with some 80 cannons. The Confederate ammunition was low, and the fire did little damage.",
        "Around 3 p.m., 10,500 to 12,500 men advanced about 1.2 kilometers across open ground. This is Pickett's Charge. The Union artillery and rifles tore holes in the line. They broke part of the line at the Angle, but reinforcements closed the gap. Armistead's brigade went farthest, near a grove of low trees. It is the high-water mark of the Confederacy. Armistead was mortally wounded, and almost half of the attackers did not come back. Pickett's division lost about two-thirds of its men.",
        { note: "Longstreet said at the time that the attack needed many more men, and in his memoirs he again criticized Lee's decision." },
        "At the same time, some five kilometers to the northeast, Stuart faced the cavalry of Gregg and Custer, in a draw that kept him from reaching the Union rear."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Between 46,000 and 51,000 dead, wounded and missing, the largest number of any battle of the war. The Union lost 23,055 men (3,155 dead, 14,531 wounded and 5,369 missing). For the Confederacy, the estimates range from 23,000 to 28,000. More generals died than in any other battle of the war, including Armistead, Garnett, Barksdale, Semmes, Pender and Pettigrew.",
        "On 4 July, under heavy rain, the armies eyed each other. Meade did not attack. At night, Lee began the retreat to Virginia. That same day, 1,500 kilometers away, Vicksburg surrendered to Ulysses Grant.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Lee's invasion was the last one. With Vicksburg, the Confederacy lost control of the Mississippi River. On 19 November 1863, Lincoln went to the dedication of the Gettysburg national cemetery and gave the famous speech, of 271 words, that redefined the purpose of the war.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The town of some 2,400 inhabitants cared for some 14,000 wounded Union soldiers and 8,000 Confederate prisoners. The only civilian known to have been killed was Ginnie Wade. Some 3,000 to 5,000 horses died and were burned south of the town." },
        { note: "The causes of the defeat have been debated for more than 150 years: Ewell and Cemetery Hill, Longstreet's delay, Stuart's absence, Sickles's advance and Meade's caution on 4 July." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  nanquim1864: {
    war: "Taiping Rebellion (1850–1864)",
    hook: "The capital of a man who said he was Jesus's younger brother fell with an explosion beneath its wall.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Hong Xiuquan was born into a Hakka family and wanted to be an imperial official. After failing the imperial examinations for the fourth time, in 1843, he read Christian pamphlets and concluded that God was his father and Jesus his elder brother. That is: he was Jesus's younger brother.",
        "The movement began in Guangxi, at the end of 1850. In January 1851, the rebels defeated imperial troops at Jintian, and Hong declared himself Heavenly King. It was the beginning of the [[Taiping|taiping]] movement, against the [[Qing|qing]] dynasty, of [[Manchu|manchus]] origin. On 19 March 1853, they took Nanjing, renamed Tianjing, 'Heavenly Capital'. According to Wikipedia, about 40,000 Manchu civilians were killed after the conquest.",
        { h: "The road to the fall" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1853", "The Taiping take Nanjing and make it their capital."],
          ["1861", "Zeng Guofan's [[Hunan Army|exército de hunan]] takes Anqing."],
          ["May 1862", "The imperials begin to besiege Nanjing."],
          ["20 December 1863", "General Li Xiucheng asks Hong to abandon the city. He refuses."],
          ["2 March 1864", "The [[siege|cerco]] is complete."],
          ["1 June 1864", "Hong dies."],
          ["19 July 1864", "The wall explodes and the city falls."]
        ]},
        "The Qing empire was doing badly and could not win alone: its regular army had failed. The task passed to Zeng Guofan, an official from Hunan who formed an army of his own with village volunteers. That army took Anqing in 1861 and, from May 1862, besieged Nanjing."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Key figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Hong Xiuquan, about 50 years old" },
        "The Heavenly King. When the food ran out, he told the people to eat what he called 'manna', which in translation appears as 'sweet dew and medicinal herbs'. He himself gathered and ate herbs in the palace grounds. He fell ill in April 1864 and died on 1 June, perhaps because of the herbs. Most authors speak of illness. Some suggest suicide by poison. He was buried without a coffin.",
        { h: "Li Xiucheng" },
        "The best Taiping general. In December 1863, he tried to convince Hong to leave the city, and the king answered that he would kill anyone who disobeyed. He took command of everything five days after Hong's death, too late.",
        { h: "Zeng Guofan and Zeng Guoquan" },
        "Zeng Guofan was the imperial commander in chief and the creator of the Hunan army. His younger brother, Zeng Guoquan, commanded the siege in the field. Zeng Guofan only reached Nanjing on 28 July, after the fall.",
        { h: "Hong Tianguifu" },
        "Hong's son, about 15 years old, succeeded his father. He escaped from the city, but was captured and executed."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The size of the Taiping garrison is very uncertain: English Wikipedia goes as far as citing 370,000 defenders, a number not confirmed by other sources we consulted. On the imperial side, some 60,000 men entered the city at the time of the final assault. The Hunan army was poorly paid and poorly fed.",
        { h: "The siege" },
        "The siege advanced in stages. In June 1863, the imperials took Jiufu Island and cut off the north bank of the Yangtze River. In the autumn and winter of 1863, Zeng Guoquan occupied positions to the southeast and southwest of the city, with navy support. On 28 February 1864, they took the Tianbao Fort, on the highest peak of Purple Mountain. On 2 March, the city was surrounded.",
        "On 14 March, Zeng Guoquan made the first assault, with ladders, and was thrown back. After that, the imperials dug ten tunnels, near the Chaoyang, Shence and Jinchuan gates. The defenders answered with [[countermines|contramina]] and raised a second wall behind the first.",
        "On 3 July, Fort Dibao, the 'Dragon's Neck', fell on Purple Mountain. From there, the imperial artillery began bombarding the city. The imperials also filled in the gap between the wall and the slope and dug tunnels about 60 meters from the wall to place explosives.",
        { h: "19 July" },
        "On the night of the 18th, Li Xiucheng sent more than 1,000 men disguised as attackers to destroy the tunnel, but they were repelled. On the afternoon of the 19th, around 1 p.m., the imperials exploded the [[mine|mina]] beneath the Taiping Gate. The explosion brought down a large stretch of the wall and killed several hundred people. The roughly 60,000 attackers entered on four fronts. By night, the imperials controlled all the gates.",
        "Li Xiucheng returned to Hong's palace, took the king's son and tried to flee through the Hanxi Gate, but the imperial troops pushed him back. That night, about 1,000 of Li's men escaped disguised as imperial soldiers, taking advantage of the looting."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Hunan army, unpaid, lost its discipline. Its soldiers killed, raped, robbed and set fires, and many civilians were treated as rebels. The city burned until 26 July. According to Zeng Guofan's reports cited in English Wikipedia, 200,000 to 300,000 people were killed in the city, but it is not clear how reliable that number is.",
        "Li Xiucheng was captured on 22 July, near Fangshan. Zeng Guofan arrived on 28 July and ordered him to write a confession. Li was executed on 7 August. Hong's body was dug up, beheaded and burned on 30 July, and the ashes were fired from a cannon. The leader Lai Wenguang fled with some 3,000 horsemen and joined the Nian rebellion, where he fought for four more years.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The fall of Nanjing ended the Taiping capital, and the rebellion, although it still fought until 1866, was lost. The most cited estimate for the dead of the whole war is 20 to 30 million people, most from famine and disease. Some authors speak of up to 100 million. It is considered the deadliest civil war in history.",
        "According to English Wikipedia, the conflict also served as a testing ground for modern firearms, and the first single-shot bolt-action rifle made in China appeared in 1864.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Hong's death: most historians accept illness, perhaps made worse by the herbs he ate. Suicide by poison is a suggested hypothesis, without proof." },
        { note: "The number of civilian dead in Nanjing and the exact size of the garrison come largely from Zeng Guofan's own reports, and we did not find an independent count in the sources consulted." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

